; Strain relief, and the reason the cord looks right at all.
;
; VerletRope excludes its anchor's CollisionObject3D from the rope's own surface
; collision — it has to, or the cord would spend every frame fighting the body it
; hangs off. The consequence is that an anchor sitting ON the shell lets the first
; segments swing straight through it, and there is no start-side equivalent of
; plug_exit_axis to hold them out (the boot properties govern the PLUG end only).
;
; So the cord is given something to emerge FROM: a short rigid stub, with the
; anchor at its tip and clear of the body. Wherever the first segment then wanders,
; it leaves the nunchuk pointing down out of a moulded boot, which is what the real
; one does. Same idiom the plug end already uses at the other end of this cord.
[node name="StrainRelief" type="MeshInstance3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, -0.0555, 0)
mesh = SubResource("CylMesh_boot")
surface_material_override/0 = SubResource("Mat_shell")

[node name="CableAttachPoint" type="Node3D" parent="."]
transform = Transform3D(1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 0, -0.062, 0)

